The embedder can keep a page from every discard but an external one, for state Chrome doesn't track: a page reached
by a form post (reloading it would send the form again), a page with an unload prompt, a page that started a
download still running. DiscardEligibilityPolicy asks one startup-registered callback,
SetEmbedderDiscardVeto(base::RepeatingCallback<bool(const PageNode*)>), right after its checks that the page is a
loaded tab and before the early answer for an external or frozen-with-growing-memory discard, and refuses a vetoed
page outright as kOptedOut, the reason Chrome already gives a page an enterprise policy keeps. Performance manager
depends on no Ardia code: ArdiaCore registers the callback (ac_discard_veto.mm) and decides what it vetoes. An
external discard (the tab menu's Sleep Tab) is never vetoed. Every other protection is Chrome's. New unit test:
TestEmbedderVetoKeepsAllButExternal, which covers the frozen-with-growing-memory discard.

